{
  "teamId": 119,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 Los Angeles Dodgers were a juggernaut from the first pitch, assembling one of the most dominant regular seasons in franchise history with a 111-51 record. Fueled by a devastating offense that scored 847 runs and a pitching staff that posted a sparkling 2.80 ERA, this team was built to steamroll the National League — and for most of the year, that is exactly what they did. Justin Turner and Trea Turner led early charges, Mookie Betts provided MVP-caliber production, and the lineup punished opponents with 212 home runs across a season that saw them go a stunning 43-11 from July through August.\n\nThe cracks in the armor, however, were not hard to find if you knew where to look. Reliever Craig Kimbrel was a recurring nightmare throughout the second half, surrendering multiple high-leverage home runs that cost the Dodgers wins they should have had in hand. The bullpen as a whole showed moments of vulnerability even as the team piled up victories at a historic pace. The month-by-month numbers told a story of a team that could win ugly, win pretty, and win in ways they had no business winning — they were that good.\n\nWhen October arrived, the Dodgers drew the San Diego Padres in the NLDS — a scrappy, pitching-first club that refused to be intimidated by the sport's most talented roster. Clayton Kershaw, the ace upon whom so many October hopes had rested for years, was touched early in Game 1, and the Padres' lineup proved relentless. The bullpen could not hold leads that materialized, and the offense sputtered at the worst possible moments, including a crushing double play from Gavin Lux that snuffed out a rally.\n\nIn the end, the Padres knocked out the heavily favored Dodgers in four games, delivering one of the most shocking upsets in recent postseason memory. A 111-win team that had looked invincible all summer was sent home before the NLCS, a reminder that October does not care about regular season dominance. The 2022 Dodgers will be remembered as one of baseball's great regular-season teams, and one of its most painful postseason disappointments.",
  "chapters": [
